{"author":{"id":"2462ded6f56967dde059994c39a506c79e39e5dfd10c5a07f07a23a5652a8353"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1774132871,"nonce":"VzIS9G6ODhptmDIpcOr8aVurJbs=","title":"Git-bug adapter: retry on repo lock with stale lock detection","message":"## Problem\n\ngit-bug takes an exclusive lock on the git repo for every operation. The watcher polls every 30s, and manual git-bug commands frequently collide with watcher polls, producing:\n\n    Error: the repository you want to access is already locked by the process pid XXXXX\n\nWorse, if the git-bug process is killed mid-operation (OOM, SIGKILL, crash), the lock file remains and blocks ALL subsequent git-bug operations until manually removed.\n\n## Proposed solution\n\nAdd retry-with-backoff logic to `GitBugTracker._run()`:\n\n1. **Retry on lock error**: If stderr contains \"already locked\", wait 1-2s and retry (up to 3 attempts). Most lock holds are sub-second.\n\n2. **Stale lock detection**: If all retries fail, check if the PID mentioned in the error is still running (`os.kill(pid, 0)`). If the process is dead, find and remove the stale `.lock` file(s) under `.git/`, then retry once more.\n\n3. **Named constants**: `LOCK_RETRY_ATTEMPTS`, `LOCK_RETRY_DELAY_S`, all in `host/constants.py`.\n\nAlso add retry logic to git-bug calls in `cli.py` (start, accept, reject, etc.) so that user-facing commands are resilient too.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] `GitBugTracker._run()` retries on \"already locked\" errors with backoff\n- [ ] After max retries, detect stale locks (dead PID) and remove them\n- [ ] Log each retry and any stale lock removal with context\n- [ ] Named constants for retry count and delay\n- [ ] Tests: lock error triggers retry, stale lock is cleaned up, live PID lock is not removed","files":null}]}